The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!watchdog_hw_running(wdd))
    kref_get(&wd_data->kref);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because if the watchdog is expected to be running (indicated by the `WDOG_HW_RUNNING` flag), but it is stopped later without calling `kref_get()`, the subsequent `kref_put()` during the close operation will lead to a kref imbalance. This imbalance can cause the character device data structure to be prematurely released, resulting in a crash on the next call to `watchdog_open()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bool hw_running;
hw_running = watchdog_hw_running(wdd);
if (!hw_running)
    kref_get(&wd_data->kref);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a local variable `hw_running` to store the result of `watchdog_hw_running(wdd)`. This ensures that the check for whether the watchdog is running is consistent throughout the function. By using this variable, the code correctly handles the case where the watchdog is not running, ensuring that `kref_get()` is only called when appropriate, thus preventing the kref imbalance and avoiding the premature release of the character device data structure.

